  "notes": "University Park. Substantially similar to 2026 but with these differences: (1) Spring Semester 2 has MATH 22 as a 3-credit course (not 4-credit MATH 110/140/22 option in 2026); (2) Semester 3 Fall does not include explicit IST 220, CMPSC 131, or MATH 21 items that were added in 2026 to fix prerequisite chains (IST 220 and MATH 21 are prerequisites for later courses but not listed as explicit plan items in 2025 bulletin); (3) Semester 4 Spring does not include explicit DS 220; (4) ENGL 15 is listed alone (not with ENGL 30H/ESL 15 options as in 2026). These appear to be genuine differences in how the 2025-2026 bulletin presented the plan vs. the 2026-2027 bulletin revision (2026 added prerequisite-chain fixes to match course requirements). All courses verified via bulletin_scraper.py for 2025. REVIEWER FIX (2026-09): re-running bulletin_scraper.py against the live 2025-26 archive found a 1cr Summer 'IST 495' item (its own real sc_plangrid row, between Semester 6 Spring and Semester 7 Fall) that had been dropped entirely from the original build -- the file previously totaled 119cr against the bulletin's own printed 'Total Credits 120-121'. Added IST 495 into Semester 6 (matching IEC-2026.json's own placement of the same course) to bring the total to 120, matching the printed range's low end. The Could-Not-Schedule warnings for MATH 22, STAT 200, SRA 231, ETI 301, ETI 302, IST 432, DS 435, IST 452, SRA 471 are a separate, genuine issue (confirmed real via planner_engine's own catalog: STAT 200 real-requires MATH 21, and several later ETI/IST/DS/SRA items chain off IST 220/DS 220/CMPSC 131, none of which this 2025 bulletin's own suggested plan lists) -- NOT fixed here since, unlike IBBH/IEBH/IBE's single MATH-21-unlocks-everything gap, IEC's chain needs IST 220/DS 220/CMPSC 131 as real prerequisite-satisfying courses that were only added starting the 2026 bulletin revision; backporting them would mean asserting the 2025 curriculum required courses its own real, contemporaneous bulletin never listed, which is a materially different (and riskier) claim than backporting a single always-existed, always-real math class. Left as an honest, disclosed prerequisite-chain gap specific to this catalog year, same treatment as this session's IBE-2022/2023 EET 101/MET 213 gap.",
